// bisect: fusion/JIT bug (interp != jit) — FIXED, kept as regress pin.
// The WORSE face of 00, and the reason that fix is a correctness fix
// and not merely a strictness one. Found by soak aug14e (hz1 fuzz
// divergence_000000, 2026-08-14), same night, same root cause.
//
// Pre-fix: interp emitted both events, jit only the first, with
//   kernel param `v`: runtime Array([1,2,2,3,1,4,5,5]) doesn't match
//   the compiled Scalar(I64) slot (typechecker static/dynamic mismatch)
//
// 00 is an ILL-typed program that the premature guard bind let through.
// This one is WELL-typed and stays well-typed after the fix — it still
// compiles. What the premature bind corrupted here is the type itself:
// the scrutinee is `any(x, y, <array>)`, i.e. `[i64, Array<i64>]`, and
// the guard `v == i64:1` bound `v := i64` before the narrowing could
// widen it. The kernel then froze the param to Scalar(I64) — faithfully
// implementing a type that was a lie — and every Array arrival bottomed
// at the ABI boundary, silently dropping the third arm's event.
//
// So the same one-line ordering bug produces two different failures:
// programs that should not compile do (00), and programs that should
// compile are miscompiled (01). Only the second is reachable by a user
// writing correct code, which is why the fix is not gated on the
// strictness question.
//
// Post-fix both engines emit both events: `v` keeps its honest union
// type, so the param is no longer frozen to a scalar.
//
// A near-miss worth knowing: the reduced form
//     {let x = i64:1; select any(x, [i64:1, i64:2]) {v if v == i64:1 => "one", _ => "other"}}
// compiles and AGREES in both engines before AND after the fix — the
// union alone is not enough. This witness is kept unreduced because the
// reductions stopped reproducing.
{let x = i64:1; let y = x; select any(x, y, [i64:1, i64:2, i64:2, i64:3, i64:1, i64:4, {let x = array::iter([i64:1, i64:2, i64:3, i64:4]); let m = x / i64:3; let f = |n| array::map([n], |i| select i {i64:0 if m == i64:0 => i64:1, _ => i64:2}); let c = count(f(i64:0)); select count(x) {i64:4 => c, _ => never()}}, i64:5]) {v if v == i64:1 => "first [v]", v if v == i64:2 => "second ", v => "third "}}
